{"name":"Rubber Band","hex":"#ce4676","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rubber band","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An elastic band made of rubber (either natural rubber or synthetic rubber)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Alternative form of rubber-band."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rubber_band"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rubber band","blocks":[{"text":"A rubber band is a loop of rubber, usually ring or oval shaped, and commonly used to hold multiple objects together. The rubber band was patented in England on March 17, 1845, by Stephen Perry. They were originally used to hold papers and envelopes. Most rubber bands are manufactured out of natural rubber as well as for latex-free rubber bands or, especially at larger sizes, a synthetic elastomer, and are sold in a variety of sizes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Rubber_bands.jpg/330px-Rubber_bands.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubber_band","wikidata":"Q256817"},{"title":"Rubber Band (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Rubber Band\" is a song by the English singer-songwriter David Bowie. It was recorded in October 1966 following Bowie's dismissal from Pye Records and helped secure him a record contract with Decca-subsidiary Deram Records, who released it as a single in the United Kingdom on 2 December of the same year. A departure from the mod-style sound of his previous releases, \"Rubber Band\" displays a style informed by vaudeville and British music hall – influenced particularly by British actor Anthony Newley. The lyrics tell the story of a man who goes off to war and, upon his return, finds his lover fell for a brass band conductor."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/1e/Rubberbandbowie.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubber_Band_(song)","wikidata":"Q778741"},{"title":"Natural rubber","blocks":[{"text":"Rubber, also called India rubber, latex, Amazonian rubber, caucho, or caoutchouc, as initially produced, consists of polymers of the organic compound isoprene, with minor impurities of other organic compounds."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Caoutchouc_naturel.jpg/330px-Caoutchouc_naturel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_rubber","wikidata":"Q131877"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/RubberBand.jpg/500px-RubberBand.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/RubberBand.jpg/500px-RubberBand.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Measuring a rubber band","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARubberBand.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Royal_Mail_band.jpg/500px-Royal_Mail_band.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Royal_Mail_band.jpg/1280px-Royal_Mail_band.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Royal_Mail_band.jpg/500px-Royal_Mail_band.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"≈10 Royal Mail rubber bands, on a letter size guide","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARoyal_Mail_band.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Ranger_bands_01.jpg/500px-Ranger_bands_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Ranger_bands_01.jpg/1280px-Ranger_bands_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campai